{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 proposes a difficulty × accuracy-differential interaction test as the single-evaluation discriminator, but this fails the school's demand for a causal mechanism. The transfer claim asserts 'genuine reliability gain is difficulty-amplifying; mere compliance is difficulty-attenuating' without any causal derivation. The analogy's own overturn conditions concede the interaction need not be monotonic, which undercuts the discriminative power. More fundamentally, if checklist features are causal but co-occur with incidental features that also correlate with outcomes, the interaction is confounded: an interaction effect could be driven by the incidental co-variation rather than by whether the checklist mechanism is genuinely tracking the outcome. No causal mechanism — e.g., an intervention on checklist coverage that would shift the outcome distribution — is specified that would rule out this confound.","target":"2d77c008c67eb8259f91a47e7061bb819ce0981bd5d006cf8dec59866f1a7495"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 claims the distinction cannot be made within a single evaluation, but its own overturn conditions concede the possibility that error covariance structure could make the hypotheses identifiable or that structural assumptions within a single evaluation could suffice. The problem asks for evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability from mere compliance; SRC_002 dismisses this as impossible yet lists exactly the kind of within-evaluation evidence that would break the indistinguishability. The coin-flip analogy overstates the case: within a single evaluation there are typically multiple items across varying difficulty and checklist coverage, not a single Bernoulli trial. An error covariance test — which SRC_002 itself admits as an overturn condition — is feasible across multiple items within one evaluation, so the core impossibility claim is not sustained.","target":"66e4f68a2a19130d535bb96edf671acd1ec625f18c276dfbf31d0d239d88a154"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 claims leave-one-out distinguishes procedure-driven reliability from member-driven reliability, but the analogy's own overturn conditions expose the missing causal mechanism. If panel judgments involve social dynamics (discussion or voting), leaving out one member changes the group's judgment process — the leave-one-out accuracy difference is then driven by process disruption rather than by whether the reliability is checklist-driven. No causal mechanism isolates the checklist-procedure effect from the group-composition effect. The test could show accuracy degradation when removing a member purely because the group's social equilibrium is perturbed, not because the checklist was merely coincidentally tracking the right answer — and conversely, a checklist-driven panel could absorb member removal with no accuracy change while still merely coincidentally tracking outcomes.","target":"e18f217f2bb3516621b1fa115d22e45e644fcaa83c4b82020c5aafd0c2e042ec"}]}